nation method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=6 && |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y &=18 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$" 13194139539638 17592186576411 false] [17592186531558 1074 "We need to solve the system of equations consisting of two lines:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=6 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $3,$ then we will get a $-3y$ term that can cancel with the $3y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=6 && |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-3y &=18 \\\\ 2x+3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}3x-\\cancel{3y} & =18 \\\\ 2x+\\cancel{3y} & =2 \\\\ \\hline (3x+2x) & =(18+2) \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=4$ back into the equation $x-y=6$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =6 \\\\ 4-y & =6 \\\\ -y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=4,\\,y=-2.$ So the coordinates of the point of intersection are $(4,-2).$" 13194139539638 1