g system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2y+1 \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y+1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539638 17592186576411 true] [17592186570708 1074 "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & -9 & -18 \\\\  &  & -3 & 3 & 18 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x+2)(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+3)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =(3)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-18,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c3/c3d41c300747f4f0f18607351f2f03241c9a789ed70fe72e05c5a385b708c4c8.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 false] [17592186570708 1074 "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $