nction is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x),$ which means that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ So, to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & -14 & 8 \\\\  &  & -2 & 10 & -8 \\\\ \\hline  & -1 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)(-{x}^{2}+5x-4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)({x}^{2}-5x+4) \\\\  & =-(x-2)(x-1)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the other roots of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)(0-1)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(-2)(-1)(-4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=1,2,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=8,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/21/21dec37151f40778b8f094d3ae68cd8716c993535e53496cf7f148817c72e345.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 true] [17592186572662 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -7 & -15 & -9 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 9 \\\\  & -1 & -4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(x+1)(x+3)=-(x+1)(x+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-1$ and a double root at $x=-3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+3)^{2} \\\\  & =-(1)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c084c0059cecb1fa33430bc3761a4e5fbe23a44f3db59d61b9f2acfc74a87fa7.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 false] [17592186572662 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -7 & -15 & -9 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 9 \\\\ \\hline  & -1 & -4 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)({x}^{2}+4x+3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(x+1)(x+3)=-(x+1)(x+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the cubic in factored form, we can tell that it has a single root at $x=-1$ and a double root at $x=-3.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+3)^{2} \\\\  & =-(1)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-3.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph takes an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c084c0059cecb1fa33430bc3761a4e5fbe23a44f3db59d61b9f2acfc74a87fa7.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 true] [17592186572668 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -3 & -4 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\  & -1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+2=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}-4(0)-2=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/de/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 false] [17592186572668 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|rrrr} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -3 & -4 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ \\hline  & -1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-{x}^{2}-2x-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+2x+2=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={2}^{2}-4(1)(2)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}-4(0)-2=-2\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/de/de0741fcb960e2b140660ac01307c8d6e69c3998fb79233ba8d17755c3d10fc0.png)" 13194139539638 17592186576411 true] [17592186572702 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\  &  & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+1}={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+3(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](image